STONE GAME III
🟢 Problem Statement

Alice and Bob are playing a game with an array stoneValue[].

Rules:

They take turns.

On each turn, a player can take 1, 2, or 3 stones from the start.

Each stone has a value.

Both players play optimally.

Score = sum of values taken.

Return:

"Alice" if Alice wins

"Bob" if Bob wins

"Tie" if equal.

🔎 Example
Input:
stoneValue = [1,2,3,7]

Output:
"Bob"


Why?

Alice picks 1+2+3 = 6
Bob gets 7
Bob wins.

🧠 Why This Is Hard?

This is not normal DP.

This is:

Game Theory + Minimax DP

We don't just maximize sum.
We maximize score difference.

🟢 Key Idea

Define:

dp[i] = maximum score difference current player can achieve from index i


Meaning:

current score - opponent score

Transition

At position i:

Player can take:

1 stone → stone[i]

2 stones → stone[i] + stone[i+1]

3 stones → stone[i] + stone[i+1] + stone[i+2]

Then opponent will play optimally from:

dp[i + taken]


So:

dp[i] = max(
   sum1 - dp[i+1],
   sum2 - dp[i+2],
   sum3 - dp[i+3]
)


Why subtract?

Because opponent's best move reduces your score difference.

🟢 Final Decision

After computing:

dp[0]


If:

dp[0] > 0 → Alice wins

dp[0] < 0 → Bob wins

dp[0] == 0 → Tie

🟢 Java Code
class Solution {

    public String stoneGameIII(int[] stoneValue) {

        int n = stoneValue.length;

        Integer[] dp = new Integer[n];

        int result = dfs(stoneValue, 0, dp);

        if (result > 0) return "Alice";
        else if (result < 0) return "Bob";
        else return "Tie";
    }

    private int dfs(int[] stones, int i, Integer[] dp) {

        if (i >= stones.length) return 0;

        if (dp[i] != null) return dp[i];

        int sum = 0;
        int maxDiff = Integer.MIN_VALUE;

        for (int x = 0; x < 3 && i + x < stones.length; x++) {

            sum += stones[i + x];

            int nextDiff = dfs(stones, i + x + 1, dp);

            maxDiff = Math.max(maxDiff, sum - nextDiff);
        }

        dp[i] = maxDiff;

        return maxDiff;
    }
}

🟢 Line-by-Line Explanation
🔹 dp array
Integer[] dp = new Integer[n];


Stores result from index i.

🔹 Base Case
if (i >= stones.length)
    return 0;


No stones left → difference = 0.

🔹 Try taking 1, 2, or 3 stones
for (int x = 0; x < 3 && i + x < stones.length; x++)


We simulate all 3 possibilities.

🔹 Core Logic
maxDiff = Math.max(maxDiff, sum - nextDiff);


If you take sum,
opponent will get nextDiff.

So your net advantage:

sum - opponent_best

🟢 Dry Run

Example:

[1,2,3,7]


Work backward:

At index 3:

dp[3] = 7


Index 2:
Take 3 → 3 - 7 = -4
Take 3+7 → 10

Best = 10

Index 1:
Try combinations
Eventually dp[1] = ?

Finally dp[0] < 0 → Bob wins.

🟢 Time Complexity

Each index computed once.

Time: O(n)
Space: O(n)
